For $a\in[0,1]^E$ define $$\label{discrete:ising-Z}
 Z(a)=\sum_{\sigma\in\{-,+\}^{V}}
             \prod_{e=uv\in E}a_e^{\mathbf 1\{\sigma_u\ne\sigma_v\}},
 \qquad 0^0=1.$$ Then $Z$ is increasing in every coordinate and is log-supermodular: $$\label{discrete:Z-lattice}
 Z(a\wedge b)Z(a\vee b)\ge Z(a)Z(b).$$

Fix a finite collection of annuli and tubes in a bounded planar region, each with a positive-width buffer and with prescribed geometric clearance. At mesh $\delta$, suppose the buffers contain no equality edge $E_0$ and no first-spin pin adverse to a requested sign. Then each request for good circuits, or connected good paths formed from a finite annular chain, has probability bounded below by a positive constant independent of $\delta$ for all sufficiently small $\delta$. The bound is uniform over feasible histories supported away from the buffers and over lattice translations preserving the clearances. Requests with the same sign may be combined by association; requests of different signs in disjoint buffers may be made successively. In an annular stack of fixed-ratio, separated slots between lattice scales $r$ and $L$, the probability of finding no requested good circuit is at most $$\label{discrete:stack}
 C(r/L)^\alpha,$$ for constants $C,\alpha>0$.*

*Moreover, conditioning a free ambient law on any deterministic collection of open-plus cells and their first-spin values increases every increasing event in $(B,\tau)$. This applies to a favorable path event in the ambient plane even when its path leaves the interior of a prescribed good contour.

Let the flat trace be zero, let $n$ be the distance from $z_0$ to its bounding skeleton, and let $z,w\in B(z_0,a)$, where $1\leq a\leq c_0n$ for a sufficiently small absolute $c_0$. With $b=\max(1,|z-w|)$, $$\label{moments:covariance-bound}
 0\leq\mathbb E[H(z)H(w)]
 \leq C\left(1+\log\frac{n}{b}\right).$$ Every smearing <a href="#moments:weights" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="moments:weights">[moments:weights]</a> in this ball therefore satisfies $$\label{moments:second-moment}
 \mathbb EY^2\leq C A^2\left(1+\log\frac{n}{a}\right).$$

Let $\mathcal S$ be the integer $1$-Lipschitz arrays on a finite connected graph, with fixed integer heights on a nonempty set of vertices. Set $$\mathcal W(h)=\mathbf 1_{\{h\in\mathcal S\}}
       x^{\sum_{\{i,j\}}|h_i-h_j|}.$$ Then $\mathcal S$ is finite, both rounded midpoints of $s,t\in\mathcal S$ belong to $\mathcal S$, and $$\label{moments:weight-midpoint}
 \mathcal W(m_-(s,t))\mathcal W(m_+(s,t))\geq \mathcal W(s)\mathcal W(t).$$ Moreover a linear statistic with $\sum|w_i|\leq A$ obeys $$\label{moments:rounding}
 \left|Y(m_\pm(s,t))-\frac{Y(s)+Y(t)}2\right|\leq A/2.$$ The same energy inequality holds with additional support constraints whenever both outputs satisfy those constraints.

Let $\mu(h)=\mathcal W(h)/Z$ be a finite probability measure on integer arrays. Partition its support into at most $J$ classes. Suppose that two arrays from the same class have both rounded midpoints in the full support, with the weight inequality <a href="#moments:weight-midpoint" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="moments:weight-midpoint">[moments:weight-midpoint]</a>. Let $Y$ be a linear statistic satisfying <a href="#moments:rounding" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="moments:rounding">[moments:rounding]</a>. If $M>0$, $M\geq A/2$, and, for every class $\mathcal S_i$, $$\label{moments:class-central}
 \mu\bigl(\mathcal S_i\cap\{|Y|\leq M\}\bigr)\geq c>0,$$ then there are $a_*>0$ and $C_p<\infty$, depending only on $J,c,p$, such that $$\label{moments:amplification}
 \mu\{|Y|>tM\}\leq4e^{-a_*t}\quad(t\geq0),
 \qquad \|Y\|_{L^p(\mu)}\leq C_pM\quad(1\leq p<\infty).$$ Outputs may belong to any class. In particular no stability of an individual class under midpoint rounding is required.

In a bounded height problem as specified above, suppose its fixed trace has absolute value at most $K$. Let $n$ be the distance from the smearing center to the bounding sites. For $1\leq a\leq c_0n$ and every finite $p\geq1$, $$\label{moments:smearing-bound}
 \left\|\sum_uw_uH(u)\right\|_p
 \leq C_{p,A}\left(1+K+\sqrt{\log(n/a)}\right).$$ The bound is uniform over the shape of the trace and applies in particular to the interior of a good cut after its complete inner height layer has been specified.

For the infinite-volume increment law furnished by Proposition <a href="#prop:local-mixing" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:local-mixing">11</a>, every zero-total smearing <a href="#moments:weights" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="moments:weights">[moments:weights]</a> satisfies $$\label{moments:plane-bound}
 \left\|\sum_uw_uH(u)\right\|_p\leq C_{p,A}
 \qquad(1\leq p<\infty).$$ The constants are uniform in $a\geq1$ and the center. The same estimate holds for the increment law in a finite free simply connected triangulation containing $B(z_0,C_0a)$, for a sufficiently large fixed $C_0$.

There is a constant $v=v(x)\in(0,\infty)$, depending only on the fixed weight $x$, such that for every pair of real zero-integral tests $f,g\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^2)$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \lim_{\delta\downarrow0}\mathbb E[H_\delta(f)H_\delta(g)]
 &=\frac{v}{(2\pi)^2}
       \int_{\mathbb R^2}\frac{\widehat f(p)\overline{\widehat g(p)}}{|p|^2}
                  \,\mathrm dp                                      \label{plane:eq:covariance}\\
 &=\frac{v}{2\pi}\iint_{\mathbb R^2\times\mathbb R^2}
          f(z)\log\frac1{|z-w|}\,g(w)\,\mathrm dz\,\mathrm dw.              \notag
\end{aligned}$$ The conclusion also holds for zero-integral tests converging in $C_c^\infty$ with a common compact support.

There is a positive, locally finite measure $C$ on $\widehat\mathbb T\setminus\{0\}$, invariant under the lattice rotations and reflections, such that every finitely supported real array $(b_u)$ with $\sum b_u=0$ satisfies $$\label{plane:eq:spectrum}
 \mathbb E\left|\sum_u b_uH(u)\right|^2
   =\int\left|\sum_u b_ue^{ip\cdot u}\right|^2 C(\,\mathrm dp).$$ There is no invariant random linear tilt term. Moreover, for some $r_0>0$ and $K<\infty$, $$\label{plane:eq:annuli}
 C\{r\le|p|\le2r\}\le K\quad(0<r<r_0),
 \qquad
 \int_{0<|p|<r_0}|p|^t C(\,\mathrm dp)<\infty\quad(t>0).$$

Uniformly for $s\in[0,\infty]$ and sufficiently small $k\ne0$, $$\label{plane:eq:poisson}
 \int\chi(k,l)Q_s(\,\mathrm dl)=D(k,s)+O(|k|).$$ For some fixed $\kappa>0$, $$\label{plane:eq:defect}
 \int_{0<|k|<\kappa}D(k,s)\,\nu(\,\mathrm dk,\,\mathrm ds)<\infty.$$ There is also $K_1<\infty$ such that, for all sufficiently small $r>0$, $$\label{plane:eq:bands}
 \nu\{r\le|k|\le2r\}\le K_1.$$

After decreasing $r_0$ if necessary, there is $K_3<\infty$ such that $$\label{plane:eq:endcaps}
 C\{b<|p|\le2b,\ |k|\le h\}\le K_3\frac hb
 \qquad(0<b<r_0,\quad0<h<b/10).$$ The same bound holds in each rotated chart. In particular, rescaling cannot create mass on one of the tangential zero lines inside a compact punctured annulus.

The bracket traces are actual integer heights satisfying $$\label{pins:trace-intervals}
 H_\delta^{+,\epsilon}\in\{0,1\},\qquad
 H_\delta^{-,\epsilon}\in\{-1,0\}
 \quad\hbox{on }S_{0,\delta},\qquad
 H_\delta^{\pm,\epsilon}=0\quad\hbox{on }A_{\delta,\epsilon}.$$ Conditional on either trace, the free interior has the original weighted height Gibbs law. There is an ordered coupling $$\label{pins:height-sandwich}
 H_\delta^{-,\epsilon}\le h_\delta\le H_\delta^{+,\epsilon}$$ on every interior face. The bounded-trace smearing estimates of Proposition <a href="#prop:smearing" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:smearing">21</a> apply to both brackets with constants independent of the window sizes.

Suppose a family of checked obstacles and factors lies strictly on one side of a site row. Their filled neighborhoods and all needed check paths lie on that side; unbounded factors have free test neighborhoods disjoint from the obstacle neighborhoods. If $A$ is a bounded product, possibly including arbitrary Haar phases, times at most one smooth zero-total insertion, then $$\label{pins:normalized-side-bound}
 \left\|A\prod_i\frac{\mathbf 1_{P_i\cap C_i}}{p_{i,\delta}}
       \right\|_\vartheta\le C.$$ The bound is uniform on compact families with the stated margins.

There is a function $\omega(a)\to0$ as $a\downarrow0$ such that $$\begin{aligned}
 \limsup_{\delta\downarrow0}
       \mathbb P(P_=^c\mid P)&\le\omega(a),
       \label{pins:offset-synchronization}\\
 \limsup_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \left\|\mu_{\delta,a}^{=}|_{\mathcal I_\delta}
              -\mu_{\delta,0}|_{\mathcal I_\delta}\right\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
       &\le\omega(a),\label{pins:increment-tv}\\
 \limsup_{\delta\downarrow0}
       \mathbb E_{\mu_{\delta,a}^{=}}|X_\delta|^p
       &\le C_p\qquad(1\le p<\infty),
       \label{pins:uniform-common-moments}
\end{aligned}$$ where $\mathcal I_\delta$ is the joint increment information in the fixed test disks. The constants in the last line are independent of all sufficiently small $a$.

Fix a finite regular grid as in Definition <a href="#def:brackets" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="def:brackets">35</a>. For every $f\in C_c^\infty(D)$, the upper and lower bracket fields satisfy $$\lim_{\epsilon\downarrow0}\ \limsup_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \left|\mathbb EH_\delta^{\pm,\epsilon}(f)\right|=0.
 \label{boundary:bracket-means}$$

Let $\mu_{a,b}$ be the law between a lower open-minus $B$ contour $a$ and an upper open-plus $B$ contour $b$. If the contours are ordered from bottom to top as $$a_1<a_0<\operatorname{supp}(A)<b_1<b_0,$$ where $A$ is an increasing $B$ and signed-status event, then $$\label{annulus:slide-cuts}
 \mu_{a_0,b_0}(A)\le \mu_{a_1,b_1}(A).$$ Old and new contours may use different classes, with edge-disjoint cells. In particular, writing $\tau_d A_T$ for the upward translate by $d$ of an increasing class-$T$ test, for $S,T\in\{Y,Z\}$ we have $$\label{annulus:slide-tests}
 P_S(A_T)\le P_T(\tau_d A_T),$$ whenever the downward-translated class-$T$ boundary contours have the required clearance from the original class-$S$ contours and the test.*


*Above a fixed open $W$ contour $\gamma$, let $\nu_{\gamma,b}$ denote the law with upper open-plus $B$ contour $b$. For $\gamma<\operatorname{supp}(A)<b_0<b_1$, the reverse slide gives $$\label{annulus:slide-white}
 \nu_{\gamma,b_1}(A)\le\nu_{\gamma,b_0}(A).$$
